  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSome early morning valley fog is expected again today, as \ntemperatures dip down into the mid-50s to mid 60s at daybreak. High \nlevel cirrus clouds will continue to overspread the area from west \nto east through the day, with GFS soundings showing the saturated \ncloud layer mainly between 20k to 35k feet agl, so a potentially \nrather thick layer. There also looks to be some scattered mid level \ncumulus clouds developing this afternoon closer to the 5-7K feet agl \nlevel. All in all, expect a mix of clouds and some filtered sunshine \ntoday. There looks to be enough sunshine and southerly warm air \nadvection to boost high temperatures into the low and mid-80s. Dew \npoints hold in the low to mid 60s, so it will feel slightly humid \nout there this afternoon. The 00z 3km NAM (which has been over done \nwith low level moisture lately) is showing a few pop up showers or \nt'storms over the Catskills and eastern Susquehanna region later \nthis afternoon, but most other CAMs are not showing this. Decided to \nhold PoPs below mentionable levels (10% or lower) today; so overall \nexpecting a dry day areawide. \n\nTonight will be mainly cloudy, and still generally dry. Can't rule \nout a few isolated to scattered showers though as the next system \ngradually approaches from the west. With more clouds around, the \npatchy fog will be more limited, likely only over the Catskill \nvalleys.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nA more established southerly flow of moisture will take hold \nover the area on Sunday. A warm front pushes through in the \nearly morning, with a chance for a few showers along and behind \nthis frontal boundary. Otherwise, it is looking mostly cloudy \nand humid on Sunday. The best instability looks to generally \nremain south and west of our forecast area, closer to the mid \nand upper level low/trough for most of the day. Late in the day,\nshower and thunderstorm chances to begin to increase as PWATs \njump up to 1.75 inches over our Finger Lakes and Central NY \nregions. MLCAPE is expected to remain 500 J/kg or less over our \nCWA and deep layer shear is trending lower (under 25 kts for \nCentral NY, up to 40 kts in NE PA). So at this time, not seeing \na widespread severe thunderstorm threat, but some of the showers\nand storms could certainly produce heavy rainfall. As the \ntrough and associated area of low pressure gradually shifts \nnorth and east toward our area, chances for rain and embedded \nthunderstorms increases Sunday night. PWATs rise between 1.8 to \n2.0 inches overnight as well, and warm cloud layer depth \nincreases to nearly 12k feet. This would increase rainfall \nefficiency due to the warm rain processes. It's too early to pin\ndown exact rainfall amounts in this convective type of pattern,\nnear the mid/upper level low.\n\nEnsemble guidance is trending slower with the main frontal passage, \nas a piece of the upper level trough and low continue to spin over \nthe Appalachians into Monday. There is uncertainty on just when a \nwest-northwest wind shift will occur (with a frontal passage) that \nwould start to decrease the moisture and instability levels. It \nappears much of Central NY and especially NE PA will remain under \nand moist and unstable atmosphere on Monday. The latest model \nguidance is showing PWATs between 1.75 to 2.0 inches lingering \nthrough at least the first half of Monday, before gradually \ndecreasing from NW to SE later in the day. With this slower trend, \nkept likely PoPs for showers and thunderstorms (with a mention of \nheavy rain) over most of the forecast area into Monday afternoon. \nThe likely PoPs fade to high chance back toward Syracuse, Penn Yan \nand Hornell where drier air may try to work in later Monday. \nTemperatures Sunday and Monday will be in the mid-70s to low 80s for \nhighs, with overnight lows in the 60s. Surface dew points will be 65-\n70 Sunday, rising into the upper 60s to mid-70s on Monday...this \nwill make it feel very muggy and humid. With this type of air mass \nin place there remains a marginal to slight risk for excessive \nrainfall and isolated flash flooding Sunday afternoon through Monday \nfor most of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nIt now appears the remnant upper level low may stall over the \nTennessee Valley Tuesday, before lifting back north and east toward \nour area mid week. Depending on the exact position of this feature \nand timing, our forecast area may see a brief drier reprieve on \nTuesday. This will depend on how far south the front makes it late \nMonday into Monday night. By Tuesday night and Wednesday it looks \nlike moisture and instability levels will be increasing once again. \nThis will keep showers likely and a chance for thunderstorms in the \nforecast Wednesday and Thursday, tapering to scattered showers and \nstorms for the end of the upcoming week. Despite the showers and \npossible thunderstorms around, it will still be partly sunny, warm \nand humid much of the week. Highs will be in the 80s, lows in the \n60s and afternoon dew points in the mid-60s t",
